Type 2 — add a row, full history.

Type 2 is the workhorse of historical tracking, and the most popular SCD type. When an attribute changes, you don't touch the old row — you expire it, and insert a new one. The dimension accumulates a full timeline of versions.

Ana moves from Madrid to Barcelona. Instead of overwriting, dim customer ends up with two rows. The first: customer key eleven-oh-one, natural key C-four-four-seven-one, city Madrid, effective from June twenty twenty-one, expiring the thirty-first of March twenty twenty-six, is-current N. The second: customer key eleven-oh-eight, the same natural key C-four-four-seven-one, city Barcelona, effective the first of April twenty twenty-six, expiry the high date nine-nine-nine-nine, is-current Y. Both rows are the same customer — same natural key — but each version gets its own surrogate key. The old row is stamped closed; the new row is open and marked current.

And this is where the surrogate key finally pays off. Because the fact joins on the surrogate, history stays correct automatically. Sales Ana made before April twenty twenty-six point at customer key eleven-oh-one — Madrid. Sales after point at customer key eleven-oh-eight — Barcelona. So "sales by region" reports each sale under the region that was true when it actually happened — the world as it was. Nothing is rewritten; the past is preserved exactly. And this is simply impossible with a natural-key join — which is precisely why warehouses insist on surrogates, back in module four.

Now, the cost. Type 2 grows the dimension — a new row for every tracked change — and it needs the control columns: effective date, expiry date, and is-current. Plus ETL that knows the expire-and-insert dance. That machinery is the very next section, and loading it is section ten. But the headline: Type 2 keeps full history by versioning rows — expire the old, insert a new one with a fresh surrogate key. Every fact stays linked to the version true at its time. It's the gold standard when history matters.
